#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114978 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72e4154b-bb7a-5029-b698-98588634d677" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Complementary Angle Given the Other Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "af3cc0f3-0971-5b26-b9bc-eda04373f225" :question/math-academy-id "e-2620" :question/problem "The angles $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are complementary angles. If $m \\angle a = 37^\\circ$, what is the measure of $\\angle b$?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/18/18d67b5d6d05c29fdf286406cdb8f1615f623c942c55d414b0655c8ac0ef242b.png)\n\nSince $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are complementary, the sum of their measures is equal to $90^\\circ$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠a + m∠b &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n37^{∘} + m∠b &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠b &= 90^{∘} - 37^{∘} \\\\\nm∠b &= 53^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114979 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6f00614-8fb3-5aed-8730-2118f31ab4d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Quantity Using Complementary Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "5532a2f6-e93e-5c85-bea6-be5961424ba3" :question/math-academy-id "e-2623" :question/problem "Calculate $x$, given that $\\angle{A}$ and $\\angle{B}$ are complementary, and that\n$m\\angle{A} = 3x, \\qquad m\\angle{B} = 4x-8^\\circ\\,$." :question/worked-solution "Since angles $\\angle{A}$ and $\\angle{B}$ are complementary, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle A + m \\angle B = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nHence, we can solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n3x + (4x - 8^{∘}) &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n7x - 8^{∘} &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n7x &= 98^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 14^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114980 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "359aa27d-6050-5c52-8c84-1da4daaa0505" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Complementary Angle Given an Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "83b57ed2-9933-51be-8e12-bfd95ea36be0" :question/math-academy-id "e-2627" :question/problem "Let $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ be two complementary angles. If $m \\angle B = 2m \\angle A$, find $m \\angle B$." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are complementary angles, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle A + m \\angle B = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nm \\angle B = 2m \\angle A\n$$\n\nwe obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠A + 2m∠A &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n3m∠A &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠A &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠B &= 2m∠A \\\\\n&= 2(30^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13c03056-9692-573f-bf7e-b938ec3a6529" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Diagram and Properties of Complementary Angles to Find an Unknown Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "0c3ef1dc-0262-5bed-9980-1672ca0e6f62" :question/math-academy-id "e-2629" :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\angle AOC$ and $\\angle COB$ are complementary angles. If $\\angle AOC \\cong \\angle BOD$ and $m \\angle COB = 50^\\circ$, find $m \\angle AOD$.\n\n![](images/60/60c48e8ce5fd289f6aa7b80cfd5a99db1adf9a0603e6162c1fb5b4dcf1a7e15d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle AOC$ and $\\angle COB$ are complementary angles, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOC + m∠COB &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠AOC + 50^{∘} &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nm∠AOC &= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\angle AOC \\cong \\angle BOD$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BOD = m \\angle AOC=40^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOD &= m∠AOC + m∠COB + m∠BOD \\\\\n&= 40^{∘} + 50^{∘} + 40^{∘} \\\\\n&= 130^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
